<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kate Sheppard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Katherine Wilson Sheppard</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kate Sheppard was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> suffragist who led the campaign for women's right to vote. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>, she emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch">Christchurch</a> and in 1887 became national superintendent for franchise and legislation of New Zealand's Women's Christian Temperance Union. She wrote pamphlets and organised three petitions to Parliament between 1891 and 1893, the last signed by 31,872 women, before the Electoral Act of September 1893 made New Zealand the first self-governing country in which women could vote in parliamentary elections. She was the first president of the National Council of Women of New Zealand, founded in 1896.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kate-sheppard.jpg" alt="Kate Sheppard" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: H. H. Clifford · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mrs._K._W._Sheppard_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kate Sheppard was born Catherine Wilson Malcolm on 10 March 1848 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>, to Scottish parents. After her father died in 1862 she lived with an uncle, a Free Church of Scotland minister at Nairn, who instilled in her the values of Christian socialism. In 1868 the family sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, arriving at Lyttelton in February 1869. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch">Christchurch</a> she joined the Trinity Congregational Church and became part of the city's intellectual life. She married the shop owner Walter Allen Sheppard on 21 July 1871, and their only child, Douglas, was born in 1880. She taught Sunday school and served on the YWCA's committee.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Her activism began after the American temperance speaker Mary Leavitt toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> in 1885. Sheppard helped found a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch">Christchurch</a> branch of the Women's Christian Temperance Union, and when petitions on women's issues were rejected she concluded that politicians would ignore women while they could not vote. In 1887 she became the union's national superintendent for franchise and legislation. A suffrage bill failed by one vote that year. In 1888 she published the pamphlet Ten Reasons Why the Women of New Zealand Should Vote, sending a copy to every member of the House of Representatives, and later Should Women Vote?</p><p>Her first petition, with 10,085 signatures, was presented in 1891, and a second, with 20,274, followed. The third, presented in 1893, carried 31,872 women's signatures, the largest petition presented to Parliament up to then. The Electoral Bill of 1893 passed the Legislative Council by 20 votes to 18 on 8 September, and Governor David Boyle signed it on 19 September. By the election on 28 November, 88 percent of women had enrolled and nearly 70 percent voted. Women could not stand for Parliament until 1919.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1896 Sheppard became the first president of the National Council of Women of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, and she edited the union's journal The White Ribbon. She spent time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, promoting suffrage there, and later withdrew from politics because of ill health while continuing to write. She revived the National Council in 1918. She died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch">Christchurch</a> on 13 July 1934, aged 86, leaving no living descendants. Her portrait has appeared on the New Zealand ten-dollar note since 1991, and a memorial to her stands in Christchurch.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kate Sheppard” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first national victory came in New Zealand, where petitions organised by <b>Kate Sheppard</b> and the Women’s Christian Temperance Union persuaded Parliament: the Electoral Act of September 1893 gave women the vote, and they cast it that November.</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>New Zealand's European electorates voted in a general election after campaigners led by <b>Kate Sheppard</b> had made it the first nation where all women had the right to vote in parliamentary elections.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-28">28 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Riccarton, in what is now <a href="/countries/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.41,-2.99&amp;year=1903" aria-label="Liverpool on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Kate Sheppard"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4848 5559h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4848 5559h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.41,-2.99&amp;year=1903">What was here in 1903? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
